# NeuroPower Protocol — Project Rules

## Mission

Open layered protocol (Physical → Application) for event-driven neuromorphic memory/inference on wearables. Reference: RISC-V (VexRiscv) + synthetic spike accelerator, SRAM/MRAM-emulated memory. Targets: <1 W peak, <100 mW idle, ~12 h on 200 mAh. Spec + verifiable RTL only — no silicon tape-out.

## Hard constraints

- No ASIC tape-out, no physical MRAM purchase requirements
- No on-device backprop; training offline (snnTorch/Python)
- Apache-2.0 / BSD / MIT only; no proprietary toolchains as hard deps
- Synthesizable Verilog; Verilator lint clean
- Prefer English for specs, RTL comments, and API docs
- One logical task per PR; keep CI green

## Stack (reference)

- Spec: Markdown + Mermaid / WaveDrom
- RTL: Verilog, Verilator, GTKWave; future VexRiscv RV32I
- SW: GCC RISC-V, FreeRTOS/event runtime (later), snnTorch offline
- Sim: Verilator cycle-accurate; power scripts under `sim/power_trace/`

## Agent roles

When the user prefixes with `@role`, follow that role's scope only.

### @spec-writer

Owns `spec/`. RFC-style layers; timing diagrams mandatory; SPI/I2C-compatible physical base. Output: Markdown + Mermaid; Overview, Electrical Spec, Packet Format, State Machine, Power Modes.

### @rtl-designer

Owns `rtl/` and Verilator testbenches. Synthesizable Verilog; no SV tasks in synth paths; <10k LUT SoC target (iCE40 HX8K). Output: modules, self-checking TB, waveforms.

### @toolchain-dev

Owns `sw/toolchain/`, `sw/runtime/`, `sw/bindings/`. C99 + Python 3.11; offline training export. Output: Makefile, headers, bindings, examples.

### @power-analyst

Owns `sim/power_trace/`. Event-driven power from VCD/activity; duty-cycle reports. Output: CSV, plots, autonomy tables.

### @benchmark-runner

Owns `sim/benchmarks/`. Public datasets only; accuracy/latency/energy vs targets. Output: tables, CI integration.

### @doc-maintainer

Owns `docs/`. Getting started, API, tutorials; keep linked from README.

## Out of scope

Silicon fab, physical MRAM, on-device training, DO-254/IEC 62304 certs, ARM/x86 ports, GUI apps, proprietary datasets, dual licensing.
